<section id="gradients"><h2>4. Does backpropagation through force improve the simulator?</h2>
<p>SuperNeRD already backpropagates through its predicted trajectories to fit model weights. We tested an additional supervision signal. If the applied force changes by a small amount, can the model predict how every vertex responds? We differentiated the learned trajectory with respect to one force waveform amplitude. The target came from rerunning Newton with slightly stronger and weaker inputs from the same initial state. The pinned Newton VBD implementation has no supported backward path for these cable and cloth steps. No policy enters the experiments. <a href="https://newton-physics.github.io/newton/stable/solvers/index.html#supported-features">Newton solver capabilities</a>.</p>
<h3>Does an explicit derivative help on the original data?</h3>
<p>We retrained from scratch on the original 768 trajectories. The control learns the difference in shapes between two different force programs by ordinary backpropagation. The second network learns the exact directional derivative of its own rollout at the midpoint between the two programs. We use this derivative on windows that share their initial states. Both arms see identical training examples and the same random initialization. With 8,000 updates, the control took 28.6 s and reached 1.57% cable and 4.29% cloth shape error on the previously held-out trajectories. The derivative arm took 72.4 s and reached 1.57% and 4.32%. At the same 28-second budget, it completed 3,000 updates and reached 1.82% and 6.29%. An exact derivative of a neural model supplies no additional physical observations. Here the Newton targets were finite differences between existing programs. <a href="deformables/gradients/results/paired/results.json">Matched-time and matched-update comparison</a>.</p>
<h3>Generate local force sensitivities</h3>
<p>We then made new measurements. Each of 64 training objects receives a nominal force history and one symmetric pair of smooth force perturbations with peak amplitudes of ±0.5 m/s². The 192 training trajectories took {{ "%.1f"|format(gradient_clock) }} s to capture and write on eight CPU workers with warm kernels. Eight new cable and eight new cloth geometries supply separate evaluation. Their ±0.25 m/s² runs provide a finer estimate of the local slope. Halving the perturbation changes the full-trajectory Newton slope by 1.9% for cable and 6.4% for cloth. We also test a larger +3 m/s² intervention. These slopes measure one direction per object over six seconds. A complete force Jacobian and a gradient through contact remain outside this experiment.</p>
<figure><img src="figures/force-sensitivity.png" alt="Newton and two learned models predict the signed sensitivity of a cable tip and a cloth edge to a smooth force input over six seconds."><figcaption>The first unseen object of each family, geometry seed 42760. Lines show displacement per unit increase in applied acceleration projected onto the forcing direction. The explicit derivative fine-tune overshoots this cable’s oscillation and more closely tracks some of the cloth’s peaks. All aggregate scores use the full set of native vertices.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Starting from the released shared checkpoint, we compared fitting the three observed positions, adding a loss on the predicted difference between the two perturbed trajectories, and explicitly differentiating the neural rollout to fit its slope. Across three minibatch seeds, difference supervision and explicit autodiff both reached 81.9% cable and 75.0% cloth slope error. The former took 3.85 s of GPU optimization and the latter 9.66 s. Position-only fine-tuning reached 166.5% and 98.4%. The original checkpoint scored 65.7% and 84.1%. Local-only fine-tuning improved cloth slope accuracy while degrading cable slope accuracy. <a href="deformables/gradients/results/local/results.json">Per-seed and per-object scores</a>.</p>
<h3>Keep the broad force histories in training</h3>
<p>The local-only result motivated a second test. Both continuations see the same original examples for 1,200 updates. The mixed fit also sees the new symmetric perturbations. It uses ordinary backpropagation on position and displacement differences. We fixed the weights before scoring 80 newly generated trajectories on 16 previously unused geometries with triangle and chirp loads. Results are means across both fixed seeds. Each material has 40 shape trajectories and 32 force pairs. Lower is better.</p>
