<p>The mixed model improves cloth force response over its parent on seven of eight confirmation geometries. Cable force-response accuracy is close across all three methods. With a moving restart at two seconds, the mixed model’s cloth shape error rises to 7.12% from the parent’s 6.96%, and response error is essentially unchanged at 87.1%. A single default checkpoint would conceal this trade-off. The mixed fit uses more teacher data and time than continuation on the original data. It changes only weights, so the forward architecture and per-step operations are identical. <a href="deformables/gradients/results/retention/scores.json">All confirmation and restart results</a>.</p>
<figure><picture><source media="print, (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)" srcset="figures/gradient-confirmation.png"><img src="figures/gradient-confirmation.gif" width="840" height="490" alt="Newton and the updated mixed-training checkpoint on the first confirmation cable and cloth."></picture><figcaption>Newton and the mixed checkpoint (training seed 271) on the first confirmation geometry (44810) for each material. Both are driven by the same descending chirp. <a href="figures/gradient-confirmation.gif">Open animation</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Can we update a model in under a second?</h3>
<p>We froze the learned oscillator dynamics and fitted the final linear layer to the same 192 trajectories with ridge regression on one CPU thread. Extracting features and fitting the position readout took 0.48 s. Adding derivatives of the features took the total to 0.65 s. On the 16 local probe objects, adding the derivative changed the cable response error from 62.49% to 62.13% and cloth from 89.80% to 89.06%. Its whole-shape errors were 1.62% and 3.46%. On the earlier chirp/triangle bank, the cable response error increased from the original checkpoint’s 55.59% to 69.56%. This is fast adaptation of an existing feature representation, including a modest gain on the targeted loads and a measurable loss of transfer. Parent training, data generation, warm-up and data loading are separate expenses. <a href="deformables/gradients/results/readout/results.json">CPU fit and errors</a> · <a href="deformables/gradients/results/readout/transfer.json">Transfer test</a>.</p>
<h3>Robot control derivatives</h3>
<p>For CartPole we differentiated the learned four-component next state with respect to applied force. We compared a residual MLP trained on the two extra perturbed Newton states to one trained on their central difference as a derivative target. Three matched seeds reduced one-step force-derivative errors from 21.3–21.8% to 19.6–20.6%, depending on the state component. Average forced 100-step angle RMSE changed from 73.71° to 72.98°, while passive error rose from 53.88° to 54.20°. CPU fitting took 1.25 s versus 2.18 s. Our separately calibrated six-parameter CartPole equations, which include the mass matrix and known integration scheme, scored 0.00042° on the same forced tests. This highlights how much a suitable dynamics representation matters on this robot. <a href="deformables/gradients/results/robot/results.json">Robot experiment</a>.</p>
<p>Sobolev training and derivative-informed neural operators have improved data efficiency on other tasks. DEFORM uses a differentiable elastic-rod model with learning for real cable dynamics. Our measurements support perturbation data and short weight updates for release rollouts. They give no evidence of a universal training-speed gain from adding a higher-order loss. <a href="https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2017/hash/758a06618c69880a6cee5314ee42d52f-Abstract.html">Sobolev Training</a> · <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.10745">Derivative-Informed Neural Operators</a> · <a href="https://proceedings.mlr.press/v270/chen25d.html">DEFORM</a> · <a href="https://github.com/maxkra15/supernerd/tree/main/deformables/gradients">Reproduction and checkpoints</a>.</p></section>
